    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d920">

                <pb facs="0725" n="643" xml:id="pg643"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 417.00/88.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The American Ambassador to Great
                            Britain to the Secretary of
                    State.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram—Paraphrase.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1911-11-02"><hi rendition="#italic">November 2,
                        1911</hi>.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>Sir Edward Grey writes me that when the foreign office first heard of the
                    proposed establishment of the Nicaraguan commission, they told Mr. Garden that
                    British claimants might avail themselves of it if they desired, but in that case
                    the foreign office must hold aloof and reserve the right to intervene
                    diplomatically if occasion arose. Sir Edward says he will have to consider
                    inviting British claimants to refer claims to arbitration if settlement can not
                    be made by direct negotiations with Nicaragua.</p>
                <p>His position therefore is that while he places no obstacle in the way of British
                    claimants who may decide to present their claims before the commission, he can
                    not accept the commission as a bar to diplomatic intervention looking to
                    arbitration. Sir Edward does not attach exaggerated importance to the questions
                    that have arisen in Central America from time to time in which our interests
                    have seemed to be divergent, for he believes that by the exercise of a little
                    patience and good will these difficulties will easily be smoothed over.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Reid.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
